+### Reduced production builds and separate validation
+
+Both rule modes can remain registered when production uses
+`build()`. Structural checks
+and build-mode rules still run before metadata is released. Validation-only
+rules remain unexecuted, and the reduced runtime discards their callbacks along
+with its inspection graph. `validation_report()` on that runtime raises
+`RuntimeError` containing `explain-metadata-disabled`; it cannot reconstruct
+the discarded graph or run validation-only checks.
+
+Use the same composition factory to create a fresh builder for validation:
+
+```python
+with application_builder().build() as production:
+    production.resolve(Application)
+
+with application_builder().build(mode="analysis") as validation:
+    validation.validation_report().assert_valid()
+```
+
+Each `application_builder()` call must return a new unbuilt builder with the same
+rules registered: builders are single-use after a successful build. The second
+build runs the mandatory startup checks, and `validation_report()` then runs the
+validation-only rules. Analysis captures rich selection history. Alternatively, use `clean-ioc check my_app.composition:application_builder`, which
+retains metadata and enables diagnostics when it builds a supplied builder.
+Run releases future overlay composition; analysis retains it. Only analysis runtimes support `validation_report()`.
+
+### Captured views and failed builds
+
+Build rules may inspect the complete supported graph, including selected/rejected decorators,
+template facts, ownership, manifests and diagnostic history. A run build temporarily captures
+that evidence whenever build rules are registered. No capability requirements are inferred
+from the rule's source or its observed reads. Validation-only registration does not enable
+this temporary capture.
+
+The `ValidationContext.graph` supplied to a run build is valid during validation and expires
+when that build finishes, successfully or unsuccessfully. This includes boundary-local views,
+saved bound methods and the view created before warmup planning. Access raises
+`RuntimeError` containing `explain-metadata-disabled`; its inspection caches are released.
+An analysis build's views remain available. Independently captured immutable reports,
+manifests and detached callback snapshots keep their existing caller-owned lifetimes.
+
+A failed run build retains the original issue code, message and dependency path, with
+captured source facts where available. A missing selection can include one
+`FailureEvidence.selection_witness`: a representative rejected candidate and its recorded
+reason, not a complete candidate history. No selection callback is replayed to recover facts.
+For unlocated rule findings, source attribution identifies the rule declaration. A saved
+finding from another graph never borrows provenance merely because occurrence numbers match.
+
+In run failures, `error.compiled_graph`, `error.report._graph` and `error.partial_graph` are
+`None`, `error.explanations` is empty, and `error.diagnostics_enabled` is false.
+`error.selection_census()` raises a clear unavailable-capability error. Triage explicitly
+reports `attempt_history_available: false`; zero retained attempts does not mean every root
+was checked. Use a fresh analysis build for complete supported histories and independent
+failure aggregation. `error.report`, `error.triage_report()` and SARIF remain usable in run mode.
+
+Failed builders remain retryable. Python tracebacks, caller-retained builders, explicitly
+saved snapshots and unrelated errors raised by callbacks retain their ordinary lifetimes;
+the run failure contract does not erase those external owners.
+
